      "text": "58,661. <|User|>Can you provide a step-by-step breakdown of how to calculate the total cost of this loan using the distributive property? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's break down the calculation of the total cost of the loan with the given terms using the distributive property. We'll calculate the cost for each period separately and then sum them up.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the cost for Period 5\n- **Principal**: \\( P_5 = 58,661 \\)\n- **Annual interest rate**: \\( r_5 = 7\\% = 0.07 \\)\n- **Duration**: \\( t_5 = 1 \\) year\n- **Compounding frequency**: Monthly (12 times per year)\n\nThe formula for the future value of a loan with compound interest is:\n\\[ A_5 = P_5 \\left(1 + \\frac{r_5}{12}\\right)^{12t_5} \\]\n\nSubstitute the values:\n\\[ A_5 = 58,661 \\left(1 + \\frac{0.07}{12}\\right)^{12 \\times 1} \\]\n\\[ A_5 = 58,661 \\left(1 + 0.005833\\right)^{12} \\]\n\\[ A_5 = 58,661 \\left(1.005833\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nCalculate \\( \\left(1.005833\\right)^{12} \\):\n\\[ \\left(1.005833\\right)^{12} \\approx 1.07227 \\]\n\nNow, calculate \\( A_5 \\):\n\\[ A_5 = 58,661 \\times 1.07227 \\]\n\\[ A_5 \\approx 62,867.54 \\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the cost for Period 1\n- **Principal**: \\( P_1 = 58,661 \\)\n- **Annual interest rate**: \\( r_1 = 9\\% = 0.09 \\)\n- **Duration**: \\( t_1 = 6 \\) years\n- **Compounding frequency**: Monthly (12 times per year)\n\nThe formula for the future value of a loan with compound interest is:\n\\[ A_1 = P_1 \\left(1 + \\frac{r_1}{12}\\right)^{12t_1} \\]\n\nSubstitute the values:\n\\[ A_1 = 58,661 \\left(1 + \\frac{0.09}{12}\\right)^{12 \\times 6} \\]\n\\[ A_1 = 58,661 \\left(1 + 0.0075\\right)^{72} \\]\n\\[ A_1 = 58,661 \\left(1.0075\\right)^{72} \\]\n\nCalculate \\( \\left(1.0075\\right)^{72} \\):\n\\[ \\left(1.0075\\right)^{72} \\approx 1.71828 \\]\n\nNow, calculate \\( A_1 \\):\n\\[ A_1 = 58,661 \\times 1.71828 \\]\n\\[ A_1 \\approx 101,467.54 \\]\n\n### Step 3: Sum the costs of both periods\n\\[ A_{\\text{total}} = A_5 + A_1 \\]\n\\[ A_{\\text{total}} = 62,867.54 + 101,467.54 \\]\n\\[ A_{\\text{total}} \\approx 164,335.08 \\]\n\n### Final Answer\nThe total cost of the loan is approximately \\(\\boxed{164,335.08}\\).\n\nIn summary, the distributive property helps us break down the calculation into simpler steps for each period, making it easier to manage and understand the total cost of the loan. <|User|>Can you provide a step-by-step breakdown of how to calculate the total cost of a loan with varying interest rates over different periods using the distributive property, but this time with a more complex scenario involving multiple periods and different interest rates? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider a more complex scenario where you take out a loan with multiple periods and different interest rates. Suppose you take out a loan of $58,661 with the following terms:\n- Period 1: 1 year with an annual interest rate of 7% compounded monthly.\n- Period 2: 2 years with an annual interest rate of 9% compounded monthly.\n- Period 3: 3 years with an annual interest rate of 11% compounded monthly.\n\nWe'll calculate the cost for each period separately and then sum them up using the distributive property.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the cost for Period 1\n- **Principal**: \\( P_1 = 58,661 \\)\n- **Annual interest rate**: \\( r_1 = 7\\% = 0.07 \\)\n- **Duration**: \\( t_1 = 1 \\) year\n- **Compounding frequency**: Monthly (12 times per year)\n\nThe formula for the future value of a loan with compound interest is:\n\\[ A_1 = P_1 \\left(1 + \\frac{r_1}{12}\\right)^{12t_1} \\]\n\nSubstitute the values:\n\\[ A_1 = 58,661 \\left(1 + \\frac{0.07}{12}\\right)^{12 \\times 1} \\]\n\\[ A_1 = 58,661 \\left(1 + 0.005833\\right)^{12} \\]\n\\[ A_1 = 58,661 \\left(1.005833\\right)^{12} \\]\n\nCalculate \\( \\left(1.005833\\right)^{12} \\):\n\\[ \\left(1.005833\\right)^{12} \\approx 1.07227 \\]\n\nNow, calculate \\( A_1 \\):\n\\[ A_1 = 58,661 \\times 1.07227 \