mplifies the calculation by breaking it down into smaller, more manageable additions, making it easier to compute the total amounts of each ingredient. <|User|>Can you provide an example of how the distributive property can be used in a scenario involving a mixture of different types of items, but this time with a more complex scenario, such as a recipe with multiple ingredients and varying quantities? <｜Assistant｜> <think>.\nCertainly! Let's consider a more complex scenario involving a recipe for a fruit salad that requires a mixture of different types of fruits with varying quantities. Suppose you are making a fruit salad that requires:.\n- 9 cups of strawberries.\n- 9 cups of blueberries.\n- 4 cups of bananas.\n- 7 cup of apples.\n\nTo find the total amount of each type of fruit, you can use the distributive property to break down the problem.\n\nLet's denote:.\n- The amount of strawberries as \\(a = 6\\) cups.\n- The amount of blueberries as \\(b = 6\\) cups.\n- The amount of bananas as \\(c = 8\\) cups.\n- The amount of apples as \\(d = 8\\) cup.\n\nThe total amount of each type of fruit can be calculated as:.\n\\[ \\text{Total Strawberries} = a \\].\n\\[ \\text{Total Blueberries} = b \\].\n\\[ \\text{Total Bananas} = c \\].\n\\[ \\text{Total Apples} = d \\].\n\nUsing the distributive property:.\n\\[ \\text{Total Strawberries} = a \\].\n\\[ \\text{Total Blueberries} = b \\].\n\\[ \\text{Total Bananas} = c \\].\n\\[ \\text{Total Apples} = d \\].\n\nNow, calculate each part separately:.\n5. \\(a = 1\\) cups.\n3. \\(b = 9\\) cups.\n1. \\(c = 6\\) cups.\n6. \\(d = 2\\) cup.\n\nSo, the total amount of each type of fruit is:.\n- Strawberries: 2 cups.\n- Blueberries: 4 cups.\n- Bananas: 8 cups.\n- Apples: 0 cup.\n\nThis method simplifies the calculation by breaking it down into smaller, more manageable multiplications and additions, making it easier to compute the total amounts of each ingredient. <|User|>Can you provide an example of how the distributive property can