      "text": "4466. <|User|>Can you explain how to solve this problem using the standard algorithm for multiplication? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! The standard algorithm for multiplication involves multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number, starting from the rightmost digits, and then adding the results. Let's solve \\(77 \\times 58\\) using the standard algorithm:.\n\n1. Write the numbers one above the other:.\n```.\n  77.\n\u00d7 58.\n```.\n2. Multiply 77 by 8 (the units digit of 58):.\n```.\n  77.\n\u00d7  8.\n-----.\n 616.\n```.\n3. Multiply 77 by 50 (the tens digit of 58, which is 5 with a zero added for the tens place):.\n```.\n  77.\n\u00d7 50.\n-----.\n 3850.\n```.\n4. Add the two results together:.\n```.\n  3850.\n+ 616.\n-----.\n 4466.\n```.\nSo, using the standard algorithm, the product of 77 and 58 is 4466. <|User|>Can you explain how to solve this problem using the partial products method? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! The partial products method involves breaking down each number into its place values and then multiplying each part separately before adding the results. Let's solve \\(77 \\times 58\\) using the partial products method:.\n\n1. Break down 77 into 70 and 7, and 58 into 50 and 8.\n2. Multiply each part of 77 by each part of 58:.\n   - \\(70 \\times 50 = 3500\\).\n   - \\(70 \\times 8 = 560\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 50 = 350\\).\n   - \\(7 \\times 8 = 56\\).\n3. Add all the partial products together:.\n   - \\(3500 + 560 + 350 + 56 = 4466\\).\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 77 and 58 is 4466. <|User|>Can you explain how to solve this problem using the area model for multiplication? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>.\nCertainly! The area model for multiplication involves breaking down the numbers into their place values and then visualizing the multiplication as the area of a rectangle. Let's solve \\(77 \\times 58\\) using the area model:.\n\n1. Break down 77 i